hi there steve here it's a sunny day  again  monday morning here in palm springs  i have on my t-shirt  master and margarita t-shirt that was  given to me  by a member from link who is also  a  professional golfer  elliot and yesterday he invited my wife  and i to  watch the  professional golf tournament that was on  here  in la quinta  and we had a wonderful day i really  enjoyed it  he picked up this t-shirt in victoria bc  at the bookstore  uh it just kind of reminds me of how i  mentioned the other day this whole  polyglot community people who study  languages they interact in different  ways they help each other  before i get going today by the way i'm  going to talk about immersion i'm going  to talk about immersion because ollie  richards who is a member of our polyglot  community  uh put out a video which said that  immersion doesn't work  and of course i had a video  one or two back where i said immersion  is the only way to go so i'm going to  talk about  our different perspectives on immersion  uh before i do that though i want to  announce something that i have already  put up on twitter and facebook which is  a bit of a  event that we're doing at link  where we're asking people to  tell me  if they had a 30-minute skype coaching  session with me  what would they want me to  explain or tell them or what would they  want to talk to me about  and uh the thing is yeah just put that  in a comment here under this video if i  had a 30 minute coaching session with  steve i would want him to talk about  this or i want would want him to help me  with this and we can talk about language  learning the  all these comments  on youtube on  twitter on facebook will be put into a  hat  and we will draw and the winner will  have a 30 a minute session with me maybe  if this works out well uh i might put  the discussion or parts of it up on uh  here on my youtube channel  and we might even do it again so um yeah  please let us know  what you would like to talk about if you  could have a chat with me about language  learning now getting back to this ollie  richards steve kaufman polyglots debate  so lo and behold  i follow different people on twitter  including ollie richards ollie richards  by the way is a great guy an excellent  polyglot speaks a variety of languages  from cantonese to arabic to spanish to  portuguese to french you name it  very helpful person very much involved  in the montreal polyglot conference and  the kind of guy  who's always willing to help other  people  as well as being good polyglot  so lo and behold here's this thing in  polish and again how the whole polyglot  community works oblong  in other words here you have pardon me  my rusty polish  but this fellow jensen  jensic marieski  said he or her that two polyglots  disagree with each other uh ollie says  um immersion is good the bad brother and  steve says it's good so  let's get to the heart of the matter  immersion you can where polygon many  polygons disagree about the edges of how  you learn a language there's very little  disagreement about certain points first  point is you can only get the language  through a lot of input  that's the crash in theory  because you don't have the language  inside you you have to bring the  language from those people who know the  language or from sources of the language  in order to get it into your brain and  you have to do it massively because  only that way is your brain going to get  used to the new language  so  ollie makes the point in his video  that you know you can't just ex you  can't just go to  kazakhstan maybe that's not a good  example but  you can't go to a place where just let  that ring go to a place and hear the  language around you or spend hours and  hours  watching television and hope that  somehow magically the language is going  to enter your brain it doesn't work that  way  so that's where we move from just  immersion to comprehensible input and  only by the way i can leave a link to  ali's video so ali says the issue the  key issue is that that  input has to be comprehensible all he  says in his video that it's possible  that through massive tens of thousands  of hours of listening to the language  just surrounded by the language that  some of it's going to penetrate  undoubtedly it does but it's not very  efficient and efficiency is so important  in language learning because time is one  of the key elements so obviously  the more efficiently you learn the  better you learn the more you're  building on things that you know  the more quickly you're going to learn  the language and the more you're going  to enjoy the process so the input has to  be comprehensible so we're really  talking about comprehensible  immersion  now what makes the immersion  comprehensible  now there are people and oli makes  reference to this in his video and i've  heard stephen krashen say it and others  that you should have input that's only a  little bit difficult for you and this is  sometimes defined as being sort of you  know 95 percent of the words and only  five percent are new  that's where i disagree i think that  once you are in content where you know  95 of it it's already too easy for you  you aren't learning very much you have  to go through lots and lots of content  to acquire new words because the key  struggle in language learning is to  acquire new words  um i think that you know if you just  have a book with nothing else to help  you perhaps you have to deal with 95  known words but the fact of the matter  is when you start into a language you  know zero words so how do you get from  zero to knowing a hundred words a  thousand words ten thousand words twenty  thousand words the vast number of words  you need to know in order to become  fluent if you are constantly dealing  first of all you can't start with  ninety-five percent known words you're  starting with 100  uh  unknown words  so therefore we now get to what's  possible nowadays with modern technology  with online dictionaries with link  with with a different you know with  audio there's a number of different ways  that can  make  uh content that has 10 or 20 unknown  words which i consider to be a bit of a  sweet spot  uh i mean obviously you started 100  unknown words but  very quickly you can move to 50 40 30  because as we know the most common words  repeat very often so as you acquire just  in any content without even trying  you're going to acquire these most  common words fairly soon and so now you  move to about 30 unknown words and and  again as i say 20  15 20 is a sweet spot where it's not too  difficult and yet you're acquiring words  at a fairly uh you know  a quick pace  so  i use link other people can use other  methods but when i'm in link i can look  up every word i can hear the word in  text-to-speech i can save a phrase and  text-to-speech i can listen to it  all of these things are helping me deal  with content so i no longer have to be  limiting myself to you know just five  percent unknown words in order for that  content to be comprehensible  the word comprehensible is a bit strong  because i'm listening to arab arabic  lessons that i don't understand more  than 10 15 20 of them until i listened  to it the 10th or 15th or 20th time but  it's comprehensible to me in the sense  that i can make it comprehensible  eventually if i stay with it  and it's compelling to me because i'm  motivated to try to understand more of  it and i can read it again and look up  the words again and listen again so as i  do this over and over again i'm  acquiring lots of new words  if i were to rely only on five percent  uh you know unknown words it would take  me forever to accumulate the vocabulary  that i need  i should add that um  you know  again on the subject of the community we  live in  skype and youtube and people and  someone recommended to me that i should  get this book called manuel dacha  boderne  okay so i ordered it from france  and it's it's an okay book there's  nothing much wrong with it lots of  grammatical explanations that kind of go  over my head or  either point out things that i've  already noticed in the language like the  adjective always comes after the noun i  mean after a couple of stories you  notice that the adjective always comes  after the noun it needn't be pointed out  as a major point of grammar in my  opinion but it's fine  um and then it has text like this but  it's difficult for me to read  uh in the book so what i've been doing  is uh  writing now in  arabic  entering these lessons into link  and then  because i got  audio cds with it i've taken each of the  lesson audios and imported into links so  i now have these as lessons in link i  wish  publishers of language textbooks  would make their texts available  digitally to those people who buy if i  buy this text i should be able to go to  a website download the audio download  the text and import it into something  like link because then this comes alive  i can read i can listen to it text to  speech i can highlight phrases i can do  all those things so that this material  becomes comprehensible and the advantage  of going to another source is as i've  said before variety is very important so  as much as i enjoy the many stories uh  you need the brain needs repetition and  novelty so it's very refreshing for me  to go to another source like this and  also do the stories there so  i guess what i'm coming to is  immersion depends how you define it  i agree entirely with with ali that if  you just go if i were to go to egypt and  surround myself with arabic i wouldn't  learn much i would still have to go back  to my room and do some deliberate  learning with sort of managed immersion  immersion that not not say 95 known  words but maybe 70 80 or  content where i at least have the the  possibility to figure out  what it all means and on that basis  immersing yourself in  content and i guess this gets back to  the point that i made a couple of videos  ago and that is don't stay with very  simple material that's too easy  get yourself into something that's sort  of lower intermediate such as our mini  stories at link such as the material in  this book  i mean even the first lesson in this  book is not that much easier than the  many stories the language if you're  talking about anything significant  happening is going to be at a level  beyond this is a book and so you may as  well get into that but get into it with  the means of understanding what's there  in other words some kind of system like  link that's going to enable you to cope  with  20 30 percent unknown words and that to  me is the level at which you can  progress quickly in the language so  in summary i don't disagree with ollie  perhaps our definitions of  comprehensible input and and even the  definition that stephen crashing has is  a little different than mine but mine is  influenced by my experience of learning  at link and don't forget if you're  interested in having a discussion with  me about language learning and you would  like me to help you on something drop a  little comment here about what you would  like to have me uh you know talk to you  about in terms of language learning the  draw will be on february the 2nd and  thereafter we'll schedule the discussion  thank you for listening and bye for now  [Music]  you
 

