y book there's  nothing much wrong with it lots of  grammatical explanations that kind of go  over my head or  either point out things that i've  already noticed in the language like the  adjective always comes after the noun i  mean after a couple of stories you  notice that the adjective always comes  after the noun it needn't be pointed out  as a major point of grammar in my  opinion but it's fine  um and then it has text like this but  it's difficult for me to read  uh in the book so what i've been doing  is uh  writing now in  arabic  entering these lessons into link  and then  because i got  audio cds with it i've taken each of the  lesson audios and imported into links so  i now have these as lessons in link i  wish  publishers of language textbooks  would make their texts available  digitally to those people who buy if i  buy this text i should be able to go to  a website download the audio download  the text and import it into something  like link because then this comes alive  i can read i can listen to it text to  speech i can highlight phrases i can do  all those things so that this material  becomes comprehensible and the advantage  of going to another source is as i've  said before variety is very important so  as much as i enjoy the many stories uh  you need the brain needs repetition and  novelty so it's very refreshing for me  to go to another source like this and  also do the stories there so  i guess what i'm coming to is  immersion depends how you define it  i agree entirely with with ali that if  you just go if i were to go to egypt and  surround myself with arabic i wouldn't  learn much i would still have to go back  to my room and do some deliberate  learning with sort of managed immersion  immersion that not not say 95 known  words but maybe 70 80 or  content where i at least have the the  possibility to figure out  what it all means and on that basis  immersing yourself in  content and i guess this gets back to  the point that i made a couple of videos  ago and that is don't stay with very  simple material that's too easy  get yourself into something that's sort  of lower intermediate such as our mini  stories at link such as the material in  this book  i mean even the first lesson in this  book is not that much easier than the  many stories the language if you're  talking about anything significant  happening is going to be at a level  beyond this is a book and so you may as  well get into that but get into it with  the means of understanding what's there  in other words some kind of system like  link that's going to enable you to cope  with  20 30 percent unknown words and that to  me is the level at which you can  progress quickly in the language so  in summary i don't disagree with ollie  perhaps our definitions of  comprehensible input and and even the  definition that stephen crashing has is  a little different than mine but mine is  influenced by my experience of learning  at link and don't forget if you're  interested in having a discussion with  me about language learning and you would  like me to help you on something drop a  little comment here about what you would  like to have me uh you know talk to you  about in terms of language learning the  draw will be on february the 2nd and  thereafter we'll schedule the discussion  thank you for listening and bye for now  [Music]  you


